sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To a given straight line in a given rectilinear angle, to apply a parallelogram equal to a given triangle.
----
proof:

Let {AB} be the given straight line, {D} the given rectilinear angle, and {C} the given triangle.

It is required to apply a parallelogram equal to the given triangle {C} to the given straight line {AB} in an angle equal to {D}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="320" height="310" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.44 diagram" src="propI44.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The payoff of the application-of-areas run: I.42 builds a parallelogram
    // equal to C in the angle D, then I.43 slides it onto AB at the right
    // length. The figure IS I.43's figure, so the colours say so:
    //
    //     I.43            I.44
    //     ABCD        ->  HLKF          (B lies on the diameter HK)
    //     EH, FG      ->  AG, ME        about the diameter  -> orange, green
    //     BK, KD      ->  LB, BF        the complements     -> cyan
    //
    // The equality chain is C = BF = LB, so the given triangle and BOTH
    // parallelograms equal to it share cyan; orange/green deliberately echo
    // I.43 so slide 7's citation reads as the same picture. The three equal
    // angles D, GBE and ABM are magenta.
    //
    // Marker economies: angle HFE and angle GFE are the SAME angle (G lies on
    // HF, so the rays coincide) — one marker, two names via alias. GBE and ABM
    // are a vertical pair at B, so both radii are pinned to 22 rather than
    // letting #103 step them apart; AHF and BHG share the arm HF, so the inner
    // one gets a smaller radius to stay distinct.
    //
    // euclid#138: A0/A3 were at x=+/-1000 (same slider-anchor pattern as I.42);
    // figureBounds() ignores visibility, so that put the bbox centre at 0
    // against a canvas centre of 160 and shoved the figure off-canvas. Pulled
    // to -40/360 -> bbox centre 160, translation ~0. G' is a second case and is
    // deliberately LEFT: it lands at (107,330), 20px below this 310px canvas,
    // and cannot be moved without changing the given angle's arm ratio, which
    // is what sets its distance. Cost is a -25px vertical shift with the whole
    // figure still visible; the library-side #138 fix retires it.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","AB","C","C1","C2","C3","D","D1","D2","D3","D1D2","D2D3","angD"];
    var APPLY = GIVEN.concat(["E","BE","AE","BEFG","G","F","BG","EF","FG","angGBE"]);
    var DRAWN = APPLY.concat(["H","HF","AH","HB"]);
    var ANG1  = DRAWN.concat(["angAHF","angHFE"]);
    var ANG2  = ANG1.concat(["angBHG"]);
    var MEET  = ANG2.concat(["K","L","M","EK","KL","AL","BM","LM","KM","HK","HLKF"]);
    var REG   = MEET.concat(["ABGH","BMKE","LABM"]);
    var CLOSE = REG.concat(["angABM"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "I.44",
        elements: [
            // Invisible anchors for the base line — see the #138 note above.
            "A0;point;free;-40,230;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;360,230;0;0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,70,0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,140,0",
            "E;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,240,0",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            // The given triangle C and the given angle D (label-only polygon
            // plus its two arms, as in I.42).
            "C1;point;free;130,40;0",
            "C2;point;free;20,40;0",
            "C3;point;free;50,130;0",
            "C;polygon;triangle;C1,C2,C3;0,0,0;0;0,0,0;cyan",
            "D1;point;free;150,90;0",
            "D2;point;free;210,90;0",
            "D3;point;free;230,30;0",
            "D1D2;line;connect;D1,D2",
            "D2D3;line;connect;D2,D3",
            "D;polygon;triangle;D1,D2,D3;0,0,0;0;0;0",
            // I.42 applied: the parallelogram on BE, angle GBE = D, area = C.
            "G';point;similar;E,B,D1,D2,D3;0;0",
            "BEFG;polygon;application;C,B,E,G';0;0;0;cyan",
            "G;point;vertex;BEFG,4",
            "F;point;vertex;BEFG,3",
            // FG produced to H, AH parallel to BG, HB joined.
            "ABGH;polygon;parallelogram;A,B,G;0;0;0;orange",
            "H;point;vertex;ABGH,4",
            // HB and FE produced meet at K; KL parallel to EA; HA and GB
            // produced to L and M.
            "K;point;intersection;F,E,H,B",
            "L;point;parallelogram;K,F,H",
            "M;point;intersection;L,K,G,B",
            // Every segment the proof names, drawn in its own right so the
            // outlines stand whichever region fills are gated off.
            "BE;line;connect;B,E",
            "BG;line;connect;B,G",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F",
            "FG;line;connect;F,G",
            "HF;line;connect;H,F",
            "AH;line;connect;A,H",
            "HB;line;connect;H,B",
            "EK;line;connect;E,K",
            "KL;line;connect;K,L",
            "AL;line;connect;A,L",
            "BM;line;connect;B,M",
            "LM;line;connect;L,M",
            "KM;line;connect;K,M",
            "HK;line;connect;H,K",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E;0;0;0",
            // The big parallelogram — invisible highlight target; its sides are
            // already drawn above.
            "HLKF;polygon;quadrilateral;H,L,K,F;0;0;0",
            // The four regions about the diameter HK. Orange and green echo
            // I.43's about-the-diameter pair; the complements are cyan.
            "BMKE;polygon;quadrilateral;B,M,K,E;0;0;0;green",
            "LABM;polygon;quadrilateral;L,A,B,M;0;0;0;cyan",
            // The three equal angles. GBE / ABM are vertical at B, radii pinned
            // equal; AHF / BHG share the arm HF, so the inner one is smaller.
            "angD;sector;angleMarker;D2,D1,D3;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angGBE;sector;angleMarker;B,G,E,22;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angABM;sector;angleMarker;B,A,M,22;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angAHF;sector;angleMarker;H,A,F,30;0;0;yellow;yellow",
            "angBHG;sector;angleMarker;H,B,G,18;0;0;orange;orange",
            "angHFE;sector;angleMarker;F,H,E,22;0;0;yellow;yellow"
        ],
        aliases: { "AG": "ABGH", "ME": "BMKE", "LB": "LABM", "BF": "BEFG",
                   "EBG": "angGBE", "GBE": "angGBE", "ABM": "angABM",
                   "AHF": "angAHF", "BHG": "angBHG",
                   "HFE": "angHFE", "GFE": "angHFE",
                   "EA": "AE", "FH": "HF", "HA": "AH", "GB": "BG",
                   "FE": "EF", "GF": "FG", "BH": "HB", "KH": "HK",
                   "LK": "KL", "MB": "BM", "ML": "LM", "MK": "KM", "EB": "BE" },
        initiallyHidden: ["HLKF","ABGH","BMKE","LABM"],
        slides: [
            { text: "Let {AB} be the given straight line, {D} the given rectilinear angle, and {C} the given triangle. It is required to apply a parallelogram equal to {C} to {AB} in an angle equal to {D}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AB","C","D","angD"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "angD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "Construct the parallelogram {BEFG} equal to the triangle {C} in the angle {EBG} which equals {D}, and let it be placed so that {BE} is in a straight line with {AB}.",
              visible: APPLY,
              highlighted: ["BEFG","C","angGBE"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "BEFG",   name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "angGBE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.42" }] },
            { text: "Draw {FG} through to {H}, and draw {AH} through {A} parallel to either {BG} or {EF}. Join {HB}.",
              visible: DRAWN,
              highlighted: ["AH","HB","HF"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "HF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend },
                  { elem: "AH", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "HB", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.2" }, { ref: "I.31" }, { ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "Since the straight line {HF} falls upon the parallels {AH} and {EF}, the sum of the angles {AHF} and {HFE} equals two right angles.",
              visible: ANG1,
              highlighted: ["angAHF","angHFE","HF"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "angAHF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angHFE", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.29" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of the angles {BHG} and {GFE} is less than two right angles. And straight lines produced indefinitely from angles less than two right angles meet, so {HB} and {FE} produced will meet.",
              visible: ANG2,
              highlighted: ["angBHG","GFE","HB","EF"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "angBHG", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.5" }] },
            { text: "Let them be produced and meet at {K}. Draw {KL} through {K} parallel to either {EA} or {FH}. Produce {HA} and {GB} to the points {L} and {M}.",
              visible: MEET,
              highlighted: ["K","KL","AL","BM"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "K",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "AL", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend },
                  { elem: "BM", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeExtend },
                  { elem: "KL", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.31" }] },
            { text: "Then {HLKF} is a parallelogram, {HK} is its diameter, and {AG} and {ME} are parallelograms about it, while {LB} and {BF} are the complements. Therefore {LB} equals {BF}.",
              visible: REG,
              highlighted: ["LB","BF","HK"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "ABGH", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "BMKE", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill },
                  { elem: "LABM", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.43" }] },
            { text: "But {BF} equals the triangle {C}, therefore {LB} also equals {C}.",
              visible: REG,
              highlighted: ["LB","BF","C"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "C.N.1" }] },
            { text: "Since the angle {GBE} equals the angle {ABM}, while the angle {GBE} equals {D}, therefore the angle {ABM} also equals the angle {D}.",
              visible: CLOSE,
              highlighted: ["angGBE","angABM","angD"],
              transition: { animations: [ { elem: "angABM", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.15" }, { ref: "C.N.1" }] },
            { text: "So the parallelogram {LB}, equal to the given triangle {C}, has been applied to the given straight line {AB}, in the angle {ABM} which equals {D}.",
              visible: CLOSE,
              highlighted: ["LB","C","angABM"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.F." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.Post.2": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post2/",
        "I.Post.5": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post5/",
        "I.15":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI15/",
        "I.29":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI29/",
        "I.31":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI31/",
        "I.42":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI42/",
        "I.43":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI43/",
        "C.N.1":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn1/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Construct the parallelogram {BEFG} equal to the triangle {C} in the angle {EBG|angGBE} which equals {D}, and let it be placed so that {BE} is in a straight line with {AB}. [!just I.42]

Draw {FG} through to {H}, and draw {AH} through {A} parallel to either {BG} or {EF}. Join {HB}. [!just I.Post.2; I.31, I.Post.1]

Since the straight line {HF} falls upon the parallels {AH} and {EF}, therefore the sum of the angles {AHF|angAHF} and {HFE|angHFE} equals two right angles. Therefore the sum of the angles {BHG|angBHG} and {GFE|angHFE} is less than two right angles. And straight lines produced indefinitely from angles less than two right angles meet, therefore {HB} and {FE}, when produced, will meet. [!just I.29; I.Post.5]

Let them be produced and meet at {K}. Draw {KL} through the point {K} parallel to either {EA} or {FH}. Produce {HA} and {GB} to the points {L} and {M}. [!just I.31]

Then {HLKF} is a parallelogram, {HK} is its diameter, and {AG} and {ME} are parallelograms, and {LB} and {BF} are the so-called complements about {HK}. Therefore {LB} equals {BF}. [!just I.43]

But {BF} equals the triangle {C}, therefore {LB} also equals {C}. [!just C.N.1]

Since the angle {GBE|angGBE} equals the angle {ABM|angABM}, while the angle {GBE|angGBE} equals {D}, therefore the angle {ABM|angABM} also equals the angle {D}. [!just I.15, C.N.1]

Therefore the parallelogram {LB} equal to the given triangle {C} has been applied to the given straight line {AB}, in the angle {ABM|angABM} which equals {D}.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
----
guide:

There are two steps in this construction. The first uses proposition @I.42 to construct some parallelogram with the correct angle equal to the given triangle. The second uses @I.43 to change its length to the proper length.

To "apply an area to a line in an angle" means just what this construction accomplishes, namely, to construct a parallelogram equal to that area with one side as the given line and one angle equal to the given angle.

In practice the angle is often a right angle. The given line may be thought of as a unit line. Then the length of the resulting rectangle represents the the area.

#### Use of Proposition 44

Besides being used in the next proposition, this construction is used in @VI.25 to make a figure similar to one rectilinear figure but equal to another.
